gakj(){return"The switch opens or closes the lighting circuit. In two-way wiring, two switches control the same lamp from two places; the intermediate switch adds a third."},
gXD(){return"The two-position selector opens or closes contact 3\u20134. Turn the lever to toggle: white stripe top-left = open, top-right = closed."},
gXm(){return"Three-position cam switch (ON, M, A markings on the face). ON: both contacts open. M closes terminals 1\u20132 (manual path, per your wiring). A closes 3\u20134 (automatic path). One click rotates the handle."},
gXA(){return"The float switch is a changeover contact. Up (floating) closes BK\u2013BN; down (hanging) closes BK\u2013BU. Click to change position."},
gXv(){return"The staircase timer keeps the lamp on for the set time after a push-button pulse. Another press restarts the time.\n\nTerminals\n- L and N: permanent supply\n- 3: push buttons, in parallel between the phase and this terminal\n- 4: switched phase to the lamps. The lamp neutral goes to the installation neutral, not to terminal N.\n\nSetting\n- Dial: \xbd, 1, 2, 3, 5, 7 or 10 minutes"},
gakq(){return"The indicator light shows the state of a circuit (running, fault...). It is wired like a lamp but draws very little current."},
gakd(){return"The network represents the supply source of the board: phase(s), neutral and earth. It is the starting point of every circuit."},
gakg(){return"The DIN rail is the standardised support for modular devices (breakers, contactors...) inside the electrical board."},
