gahb(){return"Ask me anything about your circuit: which component to pick, where to connect a wire, why it does not work."},
ga_b(){return"Your circuit has no supply: without a source, no current can flow. Add a mains supply (single or three-phase) and connect it."},
ga_a(){return"Your circuit has no load (lamp, motor, coil\u2026). An installation without a load is useless \u2014 and linking phase to neutral with no load is a guaranteed short circuit. Add at least one load."},
gatQ(){return"Current found a direct path between phase and neutral without going through any load. Nothing limits the current: it becomes huge."},
gatS(){return"The cable would heat violently in a fraction of a second \u2014 melting insulation, sparks, fire start. Protection (breaker or fuse) saves the installation by cutting immediately."},
gatR(){return"Cut the source. Set the multimeter to continuity (beep). Test between phase and neutral from the source: if it beeps when no load should link them, work back section by section until you find the extra wire or contact."},
gatT(){return u.m},
gatU(){return"Two different phases are linked directly with no load between them. Between two phases the voltage is even higher (400 V in three-phase): the fault current is even more violent."},
gatW(){return"Electric arc, molten metal splash, equipment destruction. On a real panel, this is a serious accident. Protections trip instantly."},
gatV(){return"Cut the source. In continuity mode, test each phase pair (L1-L2, L2-L3, L1-L3). A beep between two phases with no load between them = the fault. Check terminal blocks and jumpers: often a misplaced bridge."},
gatX(){return"Two phases never touch directly. Check every jumper before energizing."},
gatM(){return"Points at opposing potentials are linked without going through a load. Nothing limits the current: it becomes huge."},
gatO(){return"The cable would heat violently \u2014 melting insulation, sparks, fire start. Protection saves the installation by cutting immediately."},
gatN(){return"Cut the source. In continuity mode, test between the opposing potentials. Work back section by section to the extra wire or contact."},
gatP(){return u.m},
gatl(){return"Current cannot complete the loop: somewhere the path is broken (missing wire, open contact, unconnected terminal). The load receives nothing."},
gatn(){return"Nothing dangerous \u2014 but nothing works. This is the most common trade fault: a loose wire in a terminal block, a contact that does not close."},
gatm(){return"Two methods. Dead: continuity section by section following the current path from the phase. Live: voltmeter \u2014 voltage is present up to the break point, absent after. Where voltage \u201cdisappears\u201d, the fault is just before."},
gato(){return"Current needs a complete loop: from phase to neutral with no interruption. A single break stops everything."},
gatw(){return"This component carried a current far above what it can handle (often the result of a short circuit or missing/mis-rated protection). It is destroyed: it will no longer conduct."},
gaty(){return"Burnt smell, blackened or melted housing, sometimes smoke. A burned component is not repaired: it is replaced. And above all, find why it burned before re-energizing, or the new one will burn too."},
gatx(){return"Look at the journal: which fault came before the burnout? Fix the cause first (short circuit, missing protection), then replace the component. Never replace without finding the cause."},
gatz(){return"A burned component is a consequence, not a cause. Fix the cause first, replace afterwards."},
gatb(){return"The breaker detected a downstream short circuit and opened the circuit to protect cables and people. It did exactly its job."},
gatd(){return"This is the action that saves installations: without it, the cable catches fire. A breaker that trips is not a fault \u2014 it is a message: \u201cthere is a problem downstream of me\u201d."},
gatc(){return"Never reset without understanding. Find the fault downstream of the breaker (short circuit), fix it, then reset. If it trips again immediately, the fault is still there."},
gate(){return"Do not reset a breaker without finding why it tripped."},
ga_9(){return"The breaker tripped on thermal protection: current stayed above the rating (In \xd7 threshold, typically ~1.45 \xd7 In). This is an overload, not a bolted short circuit."},
gat8(){return"The bimetal (or equivalent) heats gradually. Too many loads in parallel, a stalled motor, or a rating too small for the installation."},
gat7(){return"Compare load current to In. Reduce the load, raise the rating if the cable allows, or fix the sizing. Then reset."},
gat9(){return"Thermal trip protects against current durably above In \u2014 size In from the load and cable cross-section."},
gat1(){return"The breaker tripped on magnetic (instantaneous) protection: current exceeded the curve multiple (B/C/D \xd7 In). Typical of inrush or a high fault current."},
gat3(){return"The electromagnet cuts immediately. Curve B is sensitive, C general, D high motor inrush. A wrong curve trips too early or too late."},
gat2(){return"Check curve and In. For motor starting: curve D or a suitable rating. If there is no normal inrush, look for a downstream fault (near short)."},
gat4(){return"Magnetic protection cuts very high currents instantly \u2014 choose the curve for the load type."},
gatg(){return"The breaker\u2019s rated operating voltage (Ue) is too low for the supply voltage. The device is not sized for this installation."},
gati(){return"Insulation and the arc chamber cannot hold. At excessive voltage: dielectric breakdown, uncontrolled arc, breaker destruction \u2014 and risk to the installation."},
gath(){return"Read Ue on the breaker nameplate. Compare it to the network nominal voltage (230 V single-phase, 400 V three-phase). Replace with a device whose Ue is at least equal to the network voltage."},
gatj(){return"Choose equipment operating voltage for the network, never below it \u2014 within the manufacturer\u2019s normalized margin."},
gasX(){return"The fault current (Icc) exceeds the breaker\u2019s rated breaking capacity (Icn). The device cannot safely interrupt this current."},
gasZ(){return"On interruption, the arc may not extinguish: breaker destruction, metal splash, fire. On a real installation this is a serious risk to people and equipment."},
gasY(){return"Read Icn on the nameplate (e.g. 6000 A). Estimate or calculate Icc at the installation point. Replace with a breaker whose Icn is greater than Icc, and check upstream selectivity."},
gat_(){return"Choose breaking capacity from the prospective short-circuit current at the installation point \u2014 never below it."},
gatI(){return"The circuit cannot stabilize: a contact that closes triggers an action that opens it, which triggers an action that closes it again\u2026 forever. The wiring contradicts itself."},
gatK(){return"The contactor \u201cchatters\u201d \u2014 rapid clack-clack-clack, accelerated wear, overheating. Classic when a contact cuts its own coil, or two contactors with poorly wired mutual interlocking."},
gatJ(){return"Follow the control logic on the diagram: is the contactor coil cut by a contact that same contactor actuates? Check cross-interlocks (KM1 NC in KM2 control and vice versa) and the NO/NC sense of each contact."},
gatL(){return"A stable control never cuts itself. Check who controls whom before wiring."},
